DEEP = {}
def d(cid, title, *paras):
    DEEP[cid] = (title, list(paras))

d("i-2", "How exile reshaped Israel's religion",
 "The Babylonian deportations came in waves: the first in 597 BC, when King Jehoiachin and the Jerusalem elite were taken; the second after the destruction of the city and Temple in 587/586 BC; a smaller one around 582. Most of the population stayed in the land. What changed was the leadership class, which now lived in Babylonia without a temple, a king or a land.",
 "That loss is the setting for much of the Hebrew Bible's final shaping. Historians widely date the editing of the books from Deuteronomy to Kings (the 'Deuteronomistic History') to this period: it reads the catastrophe as the consequence of covenant failure, not of the gods of Babylon being stronger. Prophets such as Ezekiel and the anonymous author of Isaiah 40–55 developed a sharper monotheism ('I am the Lord, and there is no other') and a hope of return.",
 "Cyrus of Persia took Babylon in 539 BC. His policy of allowing deported peoples to restore local cults (known from the Cyrus Cylinder, which does not mention the Jews) fits the biblical account of a return and a rebuilt Temple, dedicated around 515 BC. Under Persian rule Judah (Yehud) was a small province. The work associated with Ezra and Nehemiah in the fifth century made the Torah the public law of the community, tightened rules on intermarriage and rebuilt Jerusalem's walls.",
 "Two further developments mattered for Christianity later. First, a large Jewish population remained outside the land permanently, in Babylonia and Egypt (the Elephantine papyri show a Jewish garrison with its own temple on the Nile c. 400 BC): the diaspora was born. Second, the split with the Samaritans, who had their own temple on Mount Gerizim and their own form of the Torah, created the tension that the Gospels presuppose.")

d("ii-2", "How historians work on the life of Jesus",
 "Modern scholarship has gone through phases usually called 'quests'. The nineteenth-century lives of Jesus tended to produce a liberal moral teacher; Albert Schweitzer (1906) showed that each author had found his own reflection, and argued instead for an apocalyptic prophet who expected the imminent end. Rudolf Bultmann's school doubted that much could be known. From the 1950s a 'new quest' tried again with stricter tools, and since the 1980s a 'third quest' has placed Jesus firmly inside Second Temple Judaism.",
 "The tools are 'criteria of authenticity': multiple attestation (a saying found in independent sources), embarrassment (material the early Church would not have invented, such as Jesus' baptism by John), dissimilarity, and coherence with what is otherwise known. Many scholars now treat these as rough rules of thumb rather than proofs, and stress the overall pattern of memory over isolated sayings.",
 "Points of broad agreement include: Jesus came from Nazareth in Galilee; he was baptized by John; he called disciples, including an inner group of twelve; he taught about the kingdom of God, often in parables; he had a reputation as a healer and exorcist; he shared meals with people considered outsiders; he caused a disturbance at the Temple; and he was crucified in Jerusalem under Pontius Pilate around AD 30 or 33.",
 "The main disagreements concern how apocalyptic his message was, how he understood his own role (Messiah, prophet, Son of Man), and how far the Gospels' sayings reflect his words or later Christian reflection. These are historical questions; whether Jesus was also what Christians confess him to be lies outside what historical method can decide.")

d("ii-5", "Paul's chronology and the debate over his theology",
 "The firmest anchor for Paul's life is an inscription found at Delphi naming Gallio as proconsul of Achaia, which dates his term to about 51–52. Acts 18 places Paul before Gallio in Corinth, so his first stay there falls around 50–51. Working back with Galatians 1–2 (three years, then fourteen years) puts his conversion in the early to mid 30s; working forward, his arrest in Jerusalem falls in the late 50s and his death in Rome in the 60s.",
 "Seven letters are accepted by almost all scholars as Paul's own: Romans, 1 and 2 Corinthians, Galatians, Philippians, 1 Thessalonians and Philemon. They are the earliest surviving Christian writings, written roughly 50–57. 2 Thessalonians, Colossians and Ephesians are disputed; the Pastoral Epistles (1–2 Timothy, Titus) are widely regarded as later.",
 "Paul's letters were occasional, written to deal with problems, not to set out a system. The central issue in Galatians and Romans is whether Gentile believers must take on circumcision and the Jewish law. The Reformation read his 'justification by faith, not works of the law' as an answer to late medieval anxieties about merit. Since E. P. Sanders (1977) and the 'New Perspective' (J. D. G. Dunn, N. T. Wright), many scholars argue that Judaism was not a religion of earning salvation and that 'works of the law' meant above all the boundary markers separating Jews from Gentiles. Defenders of the older reading reply that Paul's contrast between grace and human achievement is still basic. The debate remains open and has ecumenical consequences, because justification divided Catholics and Protestants (see XI.6).")

d("ii-7", "Why the 'parting of the ways' is hard to date",
 "Older accounts gave a neat date for the separation of Church and Synagogue: the supposed 'Council of Jamnia' around 90, which was said to have fixed the Jewish canon and expelled Christians with a curse in the daily prayer (the Birkat ha-minim). Both claims have been heavily revised. There was no council in the formal sense, and the curse against 'heretics' (minim) was not aimed only, or perhaps at first at all, at Christians.",
 "The separation happened at different speeds in different places. The destruction of the Temple in 70 removed a shared centre. Rome's special tax on Jews (the fiscus Judaicus) forced the question of who counted as a Jew; under Nerva (96–98) it was applied more narrowly, which may have helped define Christians as a separate group in Roman eyes. The Bar Kokhba revolt (132–135), whose leader was hailed as Messiah, made it impossible for Jesus-believing Jews to join.",
 "On the Christian side, writers such as Ignatius (c. 110) already insisted that 'Christianity' and 'Judaism' were different things, and Melito of Sardis (c. 170) accused 'Israel' of killing God. Yet in the fourth century John Chrysostom was still preaching against Christians who attended synagogue and kept Jewish feasts. That suggests that ordinary believers kept crossing a line their leaders had drawn. Many historians now speak of the 'ways that never parted' cleanly, at least until Christianity gained the backing of the empire.")

d("iii-1", "The Apostolic Fathers: what each writing tells us",
 "'Apostolic Fathers' is a modern label (from the seventeenth century) for early Christian writings outside the New Testament that were thought to come from people who had known the apostles. The collection usually includes: 1 Clement, a letter from the church of Rome to Corinth (c. 96); the seven letters of Ignatius of Antioch, written on his way to martyrdom in Rome (c. 110, though some scholars argue for later); Polycarp's Letter to the Philippians and the Martyrdom of Polycarp; the Didache, a church manual; the Epistle of Barnabas; the Shepherd of Hermas, a long book of visions from Rome; the Epistle to Diognetus; and fragments of Papias.",
 "Together they show the second generation dealing with practical questions. 1 Clement argues that the Corinthians should not depose their presbyters, appealing to order and succession from the apostles. Ignatius argues for a single bishop in each church, with presbyters and deacons, as the sign of unity against teachers who denied that Christ truly suffered in the flesh (docetism). The Didache describes baptism in running water, fasting on Wednesdays and Fridays, a eucharistic prayer and travelling prophets who must be tested.",
 "These texts also show variety. Rome in 1 Clement and Hermas seems still to be led by a group of presbyters, while Ignatius already assumes a single bishop in Asia Minor. Barnabas reads the Old Testament allegorically against Jewish interpretation. Hermas allows one repentance after baptism, an early sign of the later penitential system.")

d("iii-3", "What the Apologists argued, and why",
 "The Apologists were second-century writers who addressed defences (apologiai) of Christianity to emperors, officials or educated pagans. The main names are Aristides and Quadratus (under Hadrian), Justin Martyr (two Apologies, c. 150–155, and the Dialogue with Trypho), his pupil Tatian, Athenagoras (Plea for the Christians, c. 177), Theophilus of Antioch, and in Latin Minucius Felix and Tertullian.",
 "They answered three popular charges: atheism (Christians refused to honour the gods), cannibalism (a misunderstanding of the Eucharist) and incest (a misunderstanding of 'brothers and sisters' and the kiss of peace). They argued that Christians were loyal subjects who prayed for the emperor, that their morals were higher than those of their accusers, and that punishing people for the name alone was unjust.",
 "Their more lasting contribution was intellectual. Justin used the Stoic and Platonic idea of the Logos, the divine reason, to explain Christ: the same Logos had sown 'seeds of truth' in philosophers like Socrates and was fully revealed in Jesus. This gave Christians a way to claim the best of Greek thought while asserting that it pointed to Christ. It also raised the question, pressed in the third and fourth centuries, of how the Logos relates to God the Father: is he a second, lesser god, or God in the full sense? The Arian controversy (IV.5) grew partly out of that question.",
 "Not all Apologists were friendly to philosophy. Tatian attacked Greek culture as a whole, and Tertullian later asked 'What has Athens to do with Jerusalem?' The tension between those two attitudes runs through Christian history.")

d("iii-5", "Irenaeus' answers to the Gnostics",
 "Irenaeus grew up in Smyrna, where he heard Polycarp, and became bishop of Lyon after the persecution there in 177. His five-book Against Heresies (c. 180) targeted especially the Valentinian teachers, whose systems described a series of divine emanations, a flawed creator god and a saving secret knowledge.",
 "He used three linked arguments. First, the rule of faith: a short summary of belief in one God, creator of all, in Christ who became flesh, and in the Spirit who spoke through the prophets, taught to every church at baptism. Second, Scripture: Irenaeus is the first writer to insist on exactly four Gospels and to read the Old and New Testaments as one story. Third, succession: the apostles handed their teaching to bishops in the churches they founded, who taught openly; there was no secret tradition. He illustrated this with the list of Roman bishops (see VII.1).",
 "Irenaeus' own theology centres on 'recapitulation'. Christ sums up and repeats the human story, succeeding where Adam failed, so that humanity can grow into the likeness of God. He stresses the goodness of matter and the body against the Gnostic contempt for the material world, and the resurrection of the flesh. His famous line 'the glory of God is a living human being' (Against Heresies 4.20.7) comes from this vision.")

d("iii-7", "Two kinds of monarchianism",
 "'Monarchianism' covers attempts in the late second and third centuries to protect the oneness (monarchia) of God. There were two very different kinds.",
 "Dynamic or adoptionist monarchianism held that Jesus was a man empowered by God's Spirit or adopted as Son. Theodotus the cobbler taught this in Rome around 190 and was excommunicated by Bishop Victor. Its most famous representative was Paul of Samosata, bishop of Antioch, deposed by a synod in 268. That synod is said to have rejected the word homoousios in Paul's sense, which later made the word suspect to some Easterners at Nicaea.",
 "Modalist monarchianism held that Father, Son and Spirit are three modes or names of the one God, so that the Father himself was born and suffered (hence the label 'patripassianism'). Noetus of Smyrna, Praxeas (Tertullian's target) and Sabellius taught versions of it. Hippolytus accused Bishop Callistus of Rome of sympathy with it.",
 "The responses shaped later doctrine. Tertullian's Against Praxeas (c. 213) introduced Latin terms that stuck: God is one substance (substantia) in three persons (personae), and he used the word trinitas. In the East, Origen spoke of three hypostases. 'Sabellianism' became the standard accusation against anyone who seemed to merge the three, and Nicaea's supporters were regularly accused of it.")

d("iii-9", "Ascetic Christianity before the monks",
 "Organised monasticism is usually dated to Antony and Pachomius in fourth-century Egypt (IV.10), but ascetic practice is much older. Paul already praised celibacy for those who could bear it (1 Corinthians 7). In the second century 'encratite' groups, associated with Tatian, rejected marriage, meat and wine for all believers; mainstream writers rejected that as a rule but honoured voluntary celibacy.",
 "Consecrated virgins and widows formed recognised groups in many churches. Cyprian of Carthage wrote a treatise on the dress of virgins (c. 249). The popular Acts of Paul and Thecla presented a young woman who rejects marriage to follow Paul. In Syria the 'sons and daughters of the covenant' (bnay qyama) lived celibate lives in service of the local church.",
 "Theological reasons included imitation of Christ, readiness for the coming kingdom, freedom for prayer, and an ideal of the body disciplined by the spirit. Origen is said to have castrated himself (Eusebius reports it, though some doubt it), and his teaching on spiritual ascent influenced later monks deeply. When persecution ended and the Church became respectable, many saw the ascetic life as a new form of martyrdom.")

d("iii-10", "Christianity beyond the Roman Empire before 313",
 "By the third century Christianity had spread well beyond the Greek and Latin cities. In Syriac-speaking Mesopotamia, Edessa (Urfa) was an early centre. The legend of King Abgar's letter to Jesus is late, but Christians were there by about 200: a flood chronicle records the destruction of a church building in 201, and the philosopher Bardaisan wrote in Syriac at the Edessan court. The Diatessaron, Tatian's harmony of the four Gospels, was the standard Gospel text in Syriac churches for two centuries.",
 "At Dura-Europos on the Euphrates, excavations uncovered a house converted into a church around 240, with a baptistery decorated with paintings of the Good Shepherd and the healing of the paralytic. The synagogue in the same town had even more extensive paintings, a reminder that Jews and Christians lived side by side.",
 "Armenia's tradition holds that King Tiridates III was converted by Gregory the Illuminator and made Christianity the religion of the kingdom, traditionally in 301; many historians prefer a date around 314, which would place it after Constantine. Georgia and Aksum (Ethiopia) followed in the fourth century (XIII.4).",
 "Within the Persian Empire, Christians included deportees from Roman Syria taken captive in the wars of the 250s. This community became the Church of the East (V.1). Once Rome became Christian, Persian Christians were open to the charge of being a fifth column, and the great persecution under Shapur II (from 340) followed.")

d("iii-11", "Numbers, money and synods before Constantine",
 "How many Christians were there? No census exists, so all figures are estimates. A widely discussed model (Rodney Stark, 1996) suggests growth of about 40 per cent a decade, from perhaps a thousand or so in 40 to over six million by 300, around a tenth of the empire. Other historians think the figure for 300 was lower. Christians were concentrated in cities and in the East: Asia Minor, Syria, Egypt and North Africa had far more than Gaul or Spain.",
 "Churches held property, though under Roman law their legal status was uncertain. The letter of Licinius in 313 (see the full text) orders the return of buildings 'which belonged not to individuals but to their society', which shows that churches owned assembly places. Bishop Cornelius of Rome wrote around 251 that his church supported 46 presbyters, 7 deacons, 7 subdeacons, 42 acolytes, 52 exorcists, readers and doorkeepers, and more than 1,500 widows and people in need.",
 "Bishops met in regional synods to settle disputes, a practice visible in the Easter controversy of the 190s, in Carthage under Cyprian in the 250s, and at Antioch against Paul of Samosata in 268. The bishop of the provincial capital (metropolis) gradually acquired a presiding role; Nicaea would later confirm this (VII.2). Letters between churches, carried by travellers, held this network together.")

d("iv-3", "What the 313 agreement did and did not do",
 "The name 'Edict of Milan' is misleading in three ways. It was not issued at Milan: Constantine and Licinius met there in February 313, and what survives is the letter Licinius posted at Nicomedia on 13 June 313 after defeating Maximinus Daia. It was not strictly an edict but a letter of instruction to provincial governors. And it was not the first toleration: Galerius had already ended the persecution in April 311 (IV.1).",
 "What was new was the scope. Galerius' edict tolerated Christians grudgingly, asking them to pray for the emperor. The 313 letter grants 'to the Christians and to all others' the free choice of religion, says that no religion should lose any of its honour, and orders the return of confiscated Christian property, with compensation from the treasury for buyers. Two versions survive, in Latin in Lactantius and in Greek in Eusebius; the differences are small.",
 "The letter did not make Christianity the state religion. That came only with Theodosius in 380 (IV.9). But it marked a turning point: within a few years Constantine was granting clergy exemptions from civic duties, funding churches, and summoning bishops to resolve the Donatist dispute. The language of religious freedom in the letter would be quoted again in very different circumstances in the modern period.")

d("iv-4", "How the Donatist dispute shaped Western theology",
 "The dispute began in Carthage around 311–312 when Caecilian was consecrated bishop. His opponents claimed that one of his consecrators, Felix of Abthugni, had been a traditor, someone who handed over the Scriptures during the persecution, and that the consecration was therefore invalid. They elected a rival, Majorinus, soon followed by Donatus, who gave the movement its name.",
 "The dispute came to Constantine, who referred it to a synod at Rome (313) and then a larger council at Arles (314). Both decided for Caecilian. An inquiry in 315 cleared Felix. Constantine briefly tried coercion (317–321), then gave up. The Donatists became the majority church in much of Numidia for a century, with their own bishops in most towns. Bands of rural militants called circumcellions were associated with them, though their nature is debated.",
 "Behind the quarrel lay a real theological question inherited from Cyprian: does the validity of a sacrament depend on the holiness of the minister and the purity of the Church? The Donatists said yes and rebaptized converts. Augustine, bishop of Hippo from 395, answered that Christ is the true minister of the sacraments, so a baptism given by an unworthy priest, or even outside the Church, is real, though it does not bear fruit until the person returns to unity. This is the root of the later Western teaching that sacraments work ex opere operato, 'by the work performed'.",
 "After a conference at Carthage in 411 the imperial government outlawed Donatism. Augustine came to justify state coercion with the text 'compel them to come in' (Luke 14:23), an argument later used to support persecution of heretics.")

d("iv-10", "From the desert to the Rule of Benedict",
 "Monasticism took two early forms in Egypt. Antony (c. 251–356) went into the desert as a solitary; Athanasius' Life of Antony (c. 360) made him famous across the empire and inspired many, including, according to his Confessions, Augustine. Pachomius (c. 292–346) founded communal (cenobitic) monasteries in Upper Egypt from about 320, with a written rule, common work and obedience to a superior. Between these lay the semi-eremitic settlements of Nitria and Scetis, whose teaching survives in the Sayings of the Desert Fathers (Apophthegmata Patrum).",
 "In Asia Minor, Basil of Caesarea gave monasticism a place inside the Church's life: his rules emphasised community, service to the poor and obedience to Scripture, and Basil's monastery included a hospital. Evagrius of Pontus systematised desert teaching on prayer and on the eight 'evil thoughts', the ancestor of the seven deadly sins. In Syria, more extreme forms appeared, such as Symeon the Stylite on his pillar (from c. 423).",
 "John Cassian brought Egyptian teaching to Marseilles around 415 and wrote the Institutes and Conferences. Martin of Tours founded monasteries in Gaul, and in Ireland monasteries became the main structure of the Church. The Rule of Benedict of Nursia (c. 530s), drawing on an earlier anonymous Rule of the Master, combined prayer, work and reading in a balanced community under an abbot. Through Carolingian sponsorship it became the standard rule of Western monasticism.",
 "Monks had a large role in later controversies, as supporters of Cyril at Ephesus, of Eutyches and his opponents, and of icons in the eighth century. Bishops increasingly came from monasteries, especially in the East, where bishops must be celibate.")

d("vi-2", "Why Constantinople I is counted as ecumenical",
 "The council that met in Constantinople from May to July 381 was summoned by Theodosius I and consisted of around 150 Eastern bishops. No Western bishops and no papal legates attended. Meletius of Antioch presided until his death during the council; Gregory of Nazianzus then presided briefly, faced opposition, and resigned the see of Constantinople. Nectarius, a layman not yet baptized, was chosen in his place.",
 "The council reaffirmed Nicaea, condemned the Macedonians or Pneumatomachi ('fighters against the Spirit'), who denied the full divinity of the Holy Spirit, and condemned Apollinarius. Its canons included canon 3, giving Constantinople second place in honour after Rome 'because it is New Rome', a canon Rome did not accept for centuries (VII.3).",
 "The creed now called the Nicene Creed (see the full text) is first attested in the acts of Chalcedon in 451, where it was read as the creed of the 150 fathers. No acts of 381 survive. Scholars debate whether the council composed it, adapted an existing baptismal creed, or approved it in some other form. Its new section on the Spirit avoids calling the Spirit homoousios or 'God' outright, perhaps to win over moderates, but says that he is 'worshipped and glorified together with the Father and the Son'.",
 "The council's ecumenical status came later. Rome accepted its creed in the sixth century but long rejected canon 3. Its authority rests on reception: Chalcedon read its creed alongside that of Nicaea, and later councils counted it second in the list.")
